From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Robin Snyders <robin@snyders.xyz>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 16:58:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Input: s6sy761 - restart the controller reliably on resume

s6sy761_power_on() confirms the controller came back by reading exactly one
event, at a fixed 140 ms after the supplies are re-enabled, and requiring it
to be S6SY761_INFO_BOOT_COMPLETE. Three assumptions are built into that and
none of them holds on every board.

It assumes dropping vdd and avdd restarts the part. On a board that shares
one of those supplies it does not: on the OnePlus 7T Pro avdd is the rail
that also feeds the WCN3990 Wi-Fi chip, so while Wi-Fi is up the controller
keeps its analogue supply and never restarts.

It assumes the announcement lands in one sample at a fixed delay. How long a
restart takes depends on how it was triggered, and an unrelated event may be
queued ahead of it.

It assumes the supplies are back by the time the first read happens. During
system resume they may not be, because the regulator they belong to can
still be resuming itself, and the controller answers reads with zeroes
rather than failing them while that is true.

Take an optional reset line and drive it, poll for the boot-complete event
with a bounded timeout, and retry the restart a few times before giving up.
Boards without a reset line keep the previous power-cycle behaviour and gain
the more tolerant wait.

Signed-off-by: Robin Snyders <robin@snyders.xyz>
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c | 97 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 83 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
 
 #include <linux/unaligned.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/i2c.h>
 #include <linux/input/mt.h>
 #include <linux/input/touchscreen.h>
@@ -101,6 +102,7 @@
 struct s6sy761_data {
 	struct i2c_client *client;
 	struct regulator_bulk_data regulators[2];
+	struct gpio_desc *reset_gpio;
 	struct input_dev *input;
 	struct touchscreen_properties prop;
 
@@ -288,34 +290,80 @@
 };
 ATTRIBUTE_GROUPS(s6sy761_sysfs);
 
-static int s6sy761_power_on(struct s6sy761_data *sdata)
+static void s6sy761_reset(struct s6sy761_data *sdata)
 {
+	if (!sdata->reset_gpio)
+		return;
+
+	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sdata->reset_gpio, 1);
+	usleep_range(10000, 12000);
+	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sdata->reset_gpio, 0);
+}
+
+static int s6sy761_wait_boot_complete(struct s6sy761_data *sdata)
+{
+	unsigned long timeout = jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(400);
 	u8 buffer[S6SY761_EVENT_SIZE];
 	u8 event;
 	int ret;
 
+	do {
+		ret = i2c_smbus_read_i2c_block_data(sdata->client,
+						    S6SY761_READ_ONE_EVENT,
+						    S6SY761_EVENT_SIZE,
+						    buffer);
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return ret;
+
+		event = (buffer[0] >> 2) & 0xf;
+
+		if ((event == S6SY761_EVENT_INFO ||
+		     event == S6SY761_EVENT_VENDOR_INFO) &&
+		    buffer[1] == S6SY761_INFO_BOOT_COMPLETE)
+			return 0;
+
+		msleep(20);
+	} while (time_before(jiffies, timeout));
+
+	return -ENODEV;
+}
+
+static int s6sy761_power_on(struct s6sy761_data *sdata)
+{
+	int attempt;
+	int ret;
+
 	ret = regulator_bulk_enable(ARRAY_SIZE(sdata->regulators),
 				    sdata->regulators);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	msleep(140);
+	/*
+	 * A completed boot is only announced after the controller has really
+	 * restarted, and dropping the supplies does not guarantee that: a
+	 * board may share one of them with another consumer that keeps it up.
+	 * Drive reset when the board describes the line.
+	 *
+	 * On resume the supplies may also take a while to actually come back,
+	 * because the regulator they belong to can itself still be resuming.
+	 * Until they do the controller answers reads with zeroes rather than
+	 * failing them, so retry the restart instead of giving up on the
+	 * first silent window.
+	 */
+	for (attempt = 0; attempt < 3; attempt++) {
+		msleep(20);
+		s6sy761_reset(sdata);
+		msleep(140);
 
-	/* double check whether the touch is functional */
-	ret = i2c_smbus_read_i2c_block_data(sdata->client,
-					    S6SY761_READ_ONE_EVENT,
-					    S6SY761_EVENT_SIZE,
-					    buffer);
-	if (ret < 0)
-		return ret;
-
-	event = (buffer[0] >> 2) & 0xf;
+		ret = s6sy761_wait_boot_complete(sdata);
+		if (ret != -ENODEV)
+			break;
 
-	if ((event != S6SY761_EVENT_INFO &&
-	     event != S6SY761_EVENT_VENDOR_INFO) ||
-	    buffer[1] != S6SY761_INFO_BOOT_COMPLETE) {
-		return -ENODEV;
+		dev_dbg(&sdata->client->dev,
+			"no boot-complete on attempt %d, retrying\n", attempt);
 	}
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
 
 	ret = i2c_smbus_read_byte_data(sdata->client, S6SY761_BOOT_STATUS);
 	if (ret < 0)
@@ -382,6 +430,8 @@
 	struct s6sy761_data *sdata = data;
 
 	disable_irq(sdata->client->irq);
+	if (sdata->reset_gpio)
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sdata->reset_gpio, 1);
 	regulator_bulk_disable(ARRAY_SIZE(sdata->regulators),
 						sdata->regulators);
 }
@@ -412,6 +462,12 @@
 	if (err)
 		return err;
 
+	sdata->reset_gpio = devm_gpiod_get_optional(&client->dev, "reset",
+						    GPIOD_OUT_LOW);
+	if (IS_ERR(sdata->reset_gpio))
+		return dev_err_probe(&client->dev, PTR_ERR(sdata->reset_gpio),
+				     "failed to get reset gpio\n");
+
 	err = devm_add_action_or_reset(&client->dev, s6sy761_power_off, sdata);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
--
2.50.1
